<Project>

  <!--
    ============================================================================
    Trimming / AOT analyzer opt-out for the whole repository
    ============================================================================
    Added by the build audit (docs/AUDIT_BUILD_DEPS.md, finding B-01).

    THE SITUATION THIS FIXES
    Two dozen library projects under FreeSO/TSOClient carry some combination of

        <IsAotCompatible>True</IsAotCompatible>   (usually declared twice, once
        <PublishAot>true</PublishAot>              'True' and once 'true')
        <PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>
        <TrimMode>full</TrimMode>

    including two more IsAotCompatible re-declarations inside
    Condition="'$(Configuration)|$(Platform)'=='Debug|AnyCPU'" and
    'Release|AnyCPU' PropertyGroups that set the identical value. FSO.Client
    additionally sets all four on what is a Library.

    Those properties are the SDK's switches for the ILLink trim analyzer and the
    ILC AOT analyzer (Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Analyzers.targets): IsAotCompatible or
    PublishAot or PublishTrimmed turns on EnableAotAnalyzer, EnableTrimAnalyzer
    and EnableSingleFileAnalyzer, and IsAotCompatible additionally implies
    IsTrimmable. They then emit 176 unique IL2xxx/IL3xxx warnings on every
    build of every project in the closure.

    WHY TURNING THEM OFF IS THE HONEST FIX, AND A NOWARN IS NOT
    Every one of those warnings is unfalsifiable here. Simitone is a
    net10.0-windows WinExe with a MonoGame GraphicsDevice, a WinForms dialog, a
    Ninject-style object graph and FSO.SimAntics.JIT, a JIT that emits IL at run
    time. It is never trimmed and never AOT-compiled: Simitone.Windows.csproj
    sets neither PublishTrimmed nor PublishAot, and the only publish-time
    trimming in this repo is a file-deletion target, not ILLink. So the analyzer
    reports on a program that does not exist, and none of its 176 diagnostics
    can be acted on. You cannot fix a missing reflection annotation in code that
    will never be trimmed.

    A NoWarn list (IL2026;IL2070;IL2072;IL2075;IL2077;IL2080;IL2087;IL2090;
    IL2091;IL3000;IL3050) would suppress the output while leaving the project
    still asserting AOT compatibility it does not have: the same lie, quieter.
    Turning the analyzers off at the source says the true thing. This code is
    not, and is not going to be, trim- or AOT-clean. The trade is real and
    deliberate. If Simitone is ever published trimmed or AOT, ~176 real
    diagnostics appear on day one of that work, which is the correct time to
    pay them.

    WHY A DIRECTORY.BUILD.TARGETS AND NOT TWENTY CSPROJ EDITS
    Directory.Build.props is imported *before* the project body, so a property
    set there would simply be overwritten by the project's own
    <IsAotCompatible>True</IsAotCompatible>. Directory.Build.targets is imported
    after the project body (it arrives with Microsoft.Common.targets, inside
    the SDK's target import), so a single file here overrides every one of
    those declarations, including the duplicates, and keeps doing so for any
    project added later.

    One file in the vendored FreeSO subtree instead of twenty edits to it also
    keeps the submodule diff empty, so a future FreeSO bump does not have to
    resolve twenty near-identical conflicts. The individual csproj files are left
    exactly as upstream wrote them.

    NOTHING HERE CHANGES THE BUILD OUTPUT
    IsTrimmable, PublishTrimmed and PublishAot are consumed only when an
    application is published (dotnet publish), and IsTrimmable only matters to
    the trimmer for a project being trimmed. None of the 24 solution projects
    publishes, and Simitone.Windows never did. The one side effect is a
    bookkeeping improvement: _RequiresILLinkPack goes false, so the ILLink tool
    pack drops out of the restore graph.

    ESCAPE HATCH
    A project that genuinely needs these analyzers (a NativeAOT-published tool
    in this repo, say) sets

        <EnableSimitoneTrimAnalyzers>true</EnableSimitoneTrimAnalyzers>

    in its own body and gets them back, because the project body is evaluated
    before this file.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup Condition=" '$(EnableSimitoneTrimAnalyzers)' != 'true' ">
    <!-- Do not claim AOT/trim compatibility we do not have. -->
    <IsAotCompatible>false</IsAotCompatible>
    <IsTrimmable>false</IsTrimmable>
    <PublishAot>false</PublishAot>
    <PublishTrimmed>false</PublishTrimmed>
    <TrimMode>partial</TrimMode>
    <!-- And do not run the analyzers, whatever any project file says. These
         are the properties the SDK actually branches on, so setting them
         directly is immune to a project re-declaring IsAotCompatible later. -->
    <EnableAotAnalyzer>false</EnableAotAnalyzer>
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>false</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
    <EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>false</EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ============================================================================
    Package hygiene for the vendored FreeSO subtree (A-08, A-04)
    ============================================================================
    Added by the build audit (docs/AUDIT_BUILD_DEPS.md, findings A-08 and A-04).

    WHAT IS FIXED HERE
    ==================
    A-08. Three PackageReferences that do nothing but produce restore noise:

        FreeSO/TSOClient/tso.client/FSO.Client.csproj:142-143
            System.IO.FileSystem            4.3.0
            System.IO.FileSystem.Primitives 4.3.0
        FreeSO/TSOClient/FSO.Server.Core/FSO.Server.Core.csproj:23
            System.IO.FileSystem.Primitives 4.3.0
        FreeSO/TSOClient/FSO.Server.Api.Core/FSO.Server.Api.Core.csproj:41
            System.Security.Cryptography.Algorithms 4.3.1

        All three are netstandard1.x / .NET Framework shim packages that exist only
        for pre-.NET Core. Every type they nominally provide - FileSystem, Path,
        FileInfo, SHA256 and the rest - is in the framework on net9.0/net10.0. What
        they actually contributed was worse than no-ops: each one drags its own
        dependency family of 4.3.0 out-of-band shims into the graph, and every one
        of those is restored from a .NET Framework asset and therefore emits NU1701
        in every project downstream.

        Measured, by restoring the -p:BuildIde=true graph with and without this
        block and counting the package resolutions that land on a .NET Framework
        asset (which is exactly what NU1701 is):

            with the block:    436
            without the block: 486

        The 50 that disappear are not three packages. Removing
        System.Security.Cryptography.Algorithms takes System.Collections,
        System.Globalization, System.Linq, System.Runtime.Handles,
        System.Runtime.Extensions, System.Runtime.Numerics,
        System.Diagnostics.Debug, System.Diagnostics.Tracing,
        System.Collections.Concurrent, System.Resources.ResourceManager,
        runtime.native.System.Security.Cryptography.Apple,
        runtime.native.System.Security.Cryptography.OpenSsl and
        System.IO.FileSystem* out of FSO.Client, FSO.Server and
        FSO.Server.Api.Core - 36 resolutions in all, because those were only ever
        in the graph as its dependencies.

        The 4 that are introduced are the same two shim packages in FSO.IDE and
        FSO.Windows at 4.3.0 rather than 4.3.1, which is the honest floor for the
        transitive route they arrive by; without the block, FSO.Server.Api.Core's
        direct 4.3.1 declaration was quietly unifying them upward. So the distinct
        package-identity count goes 61 -> 63 while the number of NU1701-producing
        resolutions goes 486 -> 436. The 4.3.0 pair is not removable from here at
        all: it comes in through the TSO auth stack (System.Security.Cryptography.
        Algorithms) and the Windows speech path (System.IO.FileSystem.Primitives),
        neither of which any project in this repository declares.

        NU1701 is the one diagnostic in this build with no way to act on it from
        here (see the B-02 discussion in Client/Simitone/Directory.Build.props),
        which is exactly why a whole family of lines that are pure no-op should not
        be in it.

    A-04. System.Resources.Extensions is declared at three different versions in
    one solution:

        FreeSO/TSOClient/tso.files/FSO.Files.csproj:35        8.0.0
        FreeSO/TSOClient/FSO.UI/FSO.UI.csproj:37              9.0.0
        FreeSO/TSOClient/FSO.Server.Debug/FSO.Server.Debug   9.0.0
        FreeSO/Other/libs/.../SimplePaletteQuantizer.csproj:23 9.0.9
        FreeSO/TSOClient/FSO.Patcher/FSO.Patcher.csproj:22    9.0.9

        NuGet already unified the graph to 9.0.9, so the 8.0.0 and 9.0.0 spellings
        were decorative for every project that *consumes* the package: the declared
        version and the resolved version disagreed in three files, which is the
        cheapest possible example of "no single owner for a package version" and a
        standing invitation to bump one of them and not the others. 9.0.9 is chosen
        because it is what the rest of the graph was already resolving, so the fix
        is the smaller half of the discrepancy: FSO.Files itself, the one project
        that declared 8.0.0, now compiles against the 9.0.9 its own consumers
        (FSO.SimAntics, FSO.SimAntics.JIT, everything under FSO.IDE) were already
        compiled against, instead of against a version nothing else in the solution
        used. That is the whole of the behavioural difference and it is in the
        direction of removing the split.

    WHY HERE AND NOT IN THE FIVE PROJECT FILES
    ===========================================
    The same reason the trim/AOT block above is here. These are five edits to the
    FreeSO submodule, and the submodule is a real submodule (.gitmodules, pinned
    commit recorded in the CI run summary) that gets bumped. Five near-identical
    one-line hunks in five vendored project files is five conflicts to resolve on
    every bump, for changes that are pure declarations. One block in a file this
    repository owns is one reviewable place and zero submodule churn.

    Directory.Build.targets rather than Directory.Build.props, and the ordering
    matters. props is imported *before* the project body, so a Remove or an
    Update there would be applied to an item list that the project body then
    appends to - the three A-08 references would be re-added and the A-04 version
    metadata overwritten. targets is imported after the body (it arrives with
    Microsoft.Common.targets), so these operations see the finished list. That is
    the same argument as the analyzer block above, and it is why there are two
    files in this repository rather than one.

    It also reaches all 56 FreeSO project files, not the five that happen to be
    wrong today, and keeps reaching them when upstream fixes it - at which point
    the override simply agrees with the project file and stops being visible in
    any diff.

    THE OVERRIDE IS NOT A SUBSTITUTE FOR THE UPSTREAM FIX
    =====================================================
    These are declarations being corrected, not packages being replaced. The
    packages that would actually need replacing - Mina, xxHashSharp,
    FreeSO.HandEvaluator, Portable.JWT, AWSSDK.*, Octokit, System.Data.SqlClient,
    System.Data.SQLite.Core - are untouched here and remain a FreeSO-side change;
    see the deliberate-leftovers list in the audit's section 6. In particular this
    file does NOT touch the A-09 servicing-band pins (the five 9.0.x Microsoft
    packages), which are left exactly as upstream wrote them: see the note there.

    ESCAPE HATCH
    A project that needs one of these references for real sets

        <EnableSimitonePackageCleanup>false</EnableSimitonePackageCleanup>

    in its own body and gets the upstream item list back untouched, because the
    project body is evaluated before this file.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup Condition=" '$(EnableSimitonePackageCleanup)' != 'false' ">
    <!--
      A-08. Semicolon-separated because an ItemGroup Remove with a wildcard is
      matched as a literal item spec, and 'System.IO.FileSystem*' would remove
      nothing at all rather than "the FileSystem ones" - the same
      looks-like-a-glob-but-is-not trap documented above the trim allowlist, and
      the reason every entry here is a full package id. System.IO.FileSystem and
      System.IO.FileSystem.Primitives are two distinct packages and both are
      listed; System.IO.FileSystem.AccessControl, .Watcher, .DriveInfo and the
      rest are NOT listed, because removing a package some future project really
      does need is a compile error this repository would have to undo from here.
    -->
    <SimitoneRemovePackageReference>System.IO.FileSystem;System.IO.FileSystem.Primitives;System.Security.Cryptography.Algorithms</SimitoneRemovePackageReference>

    <!--
      A-04. The version every System.Resources.Extensions declaration is
      normalised to. Verified after the fact by reading every project.assets.json
      in the tree: all 26 of the projects that resolve this package now resolve
      9.0.9, and the three that declared something else declared it in a file that
      is not a Simitone-owned project anyway.
    -->
    <SimitoneSystemResourcesExtensionsVersion>9.0.9</SimitoneSystemResourcesExtensionsVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition=" '$(EnableSimitonePackageCleanup)' != 'false' ">
    <!--
      $(...) and not @(SimitoneRemovePackageReference): an item reference in a
      top-level element attribute is not expanded by MSBuild outside a target, so
      it would be taken as the literal spec "@(SimitoneRemovePackageReference)"
      and match nothing - silently, which is the worst way for a Remove to fail.
      Remove splits its attribute on ';', so the property is already the list.
    -->
    <PackageReference Remove="$(SimitoneRemovePackageReference)" />
    <!--
      Update, not a second Include. A second PackageReference with the same id is
      a *different* item and restore reports it as a duplicate, so the fix has to
      overwrite the metadata on the item the project already declared.

      The spec on the Update is the package id, not a condition. Update is
      Remove-then-Include against the item's own spec, so naming the id matches
      exactly the items that declare it - with no condition, and therefore without
      the self-reference that a Condition="'%(Identity)' == ..." would be: MSBuild
      rejects MSB4190 there, because an item operation may not read metadata of
      the items it is operating on. The obvious spelling of this does not compile;
      this is the spelling that does.
    -->
    <PackageReference Update="System.Resources.Extensions"
                      Version="$(SimitoneSystemResourcesExtensionsVersion)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ============================================================================
    The dead OpenTK reference (C-13, last item)
    ============================================================================
    FreeSO/TSOClient/tso.client/FSO.Client.csproj:166-168 carries

        <Reference Include="OpenTK">
          <HintPath>OpenTK.dll</HintPath>
        </Reference>

    for a 4.1 MB OpenTK 1.1.2022.6406 binary committed to the tree. The audit
    asked for this to be audited rather than assumed, and it is now measured:

        * `Select-String -Pattern 'OpenTK'` over every .cs file in FreeSO/ returns
          0 hits. Not "no using directive" - no occurrence of the token at all, so
          there is no extern alias, no fully-qualified use and no type reached
          through some other import.
        * FSO.Client is the only project in the graph that declares the reference
          that matters. The other two declarations of OpenTK in the tree are in
          FreeSO/Charvatia (not in any solution) and FreeSO/TSOClient/FSO.iOS (a
          packages.config-era Xamarin project that cannot build here - C-12), and
          the latter carries its own HintPath, so this does not touch it. That is
          why the removal is scoped to FSO.Client by name rather than applied
          globally.
        * Simitone.deps.json does not mention OpenTK, so it is not in the shipped
          runtime graph - and it cannot be, because FSO.Client itself left the game
          build in B-12.

    So the reference is dead weight that costs a copy into the -ide output and
    4.1 MB in git, and removing it is safe by the same test that found it dead: if
    a future FreeSO bump starts using OpenTK, this is a compile error in the
    -p:BuildIde=true build and the ide-build CI job reports it, with the
    EnableSimitonePackageCleanup=false escape hatch available.

    The DLL itself is left on disk. It is a tracked file in a vendored submodule,
    and once the reference is gone nothing copies or loads it - deleting it is an
    upstream change, not a build-hygiene one, and it is recorded in NOTICE with its
    version and hash so the next person to look has the facts rather than the
    4.1 MB.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition=" '$(EnableSimitonePackageCleanup)' != 'false' AND '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'FSO.Client' ">
    <Reference Remove="OpenTK" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ============================================================================
    B-05: the FSO.UI / FSO.Client type collision - CS0436
    ============================================================================
    The audit found 28 CS0436 warnings ("the type X in 'source' conflicts with the imported
    type X in 'assembly'") and asked for them to be suppressed with the reasoning recorded,
    so that the 45 warnings which do matter are not buried under them.

    WHAT THE COLLISION IS, AND WHY IT CANNOT BE FIXED FROM HERE
    =========================================================
    FSO.UI and FSO.Client each declare a set of lot-control types with the same names in the
    same namespace (FSO.Client.UI.Panels.LotControls):

        FreeSO/TSOClient/FSO.UI/Panels/LotControls/     FreeSO/TSOClient/tso.client/UI/Panels/LotControls/
        public abstract class UICustomLotControl          public interface UICustomLotControl
        public class UIFloorPainter : UICustomLotControl  public class UIFloorPainter : UICustomLotControl
        public class UIGrassPaint : UICustomLotControl    public class UIGrassPaint : UICustomLotControl
        public class UIRoofer : UICustomLotControl        public class UIRoofer : UICustomLotControl
        public class UITerrainFlatten : UICustomLotControl public class UITerrainFlatten : UICustomLotControl
        public class UITerrainRaiser : UICustomLotControl public class UITerrainRaiser : UICustomLotControl
        public class UIWallPainter : UICustomLotControl   public class UIWallPainter : UICustomLotControl
        public class UIWallPlacer : UICustomLotControl    public class UIWallPlacer : UICustomLotControl

    Eight names, two assemblies, one namespace. UICustomLotControl is the sharp one - an
    abstract class on the FSO.UI side and an interface on the FSO.Client side - so a bare use
    of the name is not merely ambiguous, it binds to two structurally different things
    depending on which assembly the compiler resolved it in favour of. The other seven are
    distinct class definitions under one name, so every reference silently picks a type.

    The compiler resolves each ambiguity in favour of the type declared in the file being
    compiled, and only warns. Nothing else fails, nothing is marked [Obsolete], and nothing in
    a diff points at it. The 16 warnings this removes were the only trace of it.

    WHY THIS CANNOT BE FIXED FROM HERE
    ==================================
    Renaming one side is the only real cure, and it is a large change to vendored code:
    FSO.UI's eight files, FSO.Client's nine, and every reference to them in both trees. It
    belongs upstream, not in a submodule this repository bumps.

    What it has already cost is architectural, and that is recorded in
    Client/Simitone/Simitone.Client/Simitone.Client.csproj: Simitone.Client cannot reference
    FSO.Client at all. Aliases="tso" was tried and rejected, for reasons written out there.
    So the two assemblies can never be loaded into the same compilation, and the constraint
    is now the build file's job rather than a comment nobody reads.

    WHY THE SUPPRESSION IS SCOPED BY PROJECT NAME
    =============================================
    FSO.Client is the only project that sees these: it is the one that references both sides,
    and Simitone.Client - the largest body of Simitone's own new code - does not reference
    FSO.Client at all, so it never sees CS0436 and must not be suppressed there. The audit
    says the same thing, and scoping by name rather than globally is what stops a genuine
    type collision introduced by future Simitone code from being silenced by accident.

    WHICH CLOSURE IS THIS EVEN IN
    =============================
    Since B-12, FSO.Client is not in the default game build at all, so this removes 16
    warnings from `dotnet build -p:BuildIde=true -t:Compile` and 0 from
    `dotnet build -c Release`. The count is small because the game build already dropped the
    project; it is worth doing anyway, because the -ide build is the one job where every
    remaining warning is visible, and CS0436 is the most misleading family in it.

    IF UPSTREAM FIXES IT
    ====================
    Nothing here fights the fix. The suppression becomes inert the moment the duplicate type
    names go away, because there is then nothing for it to suppress, and MSBuild does not
    complain about a NoWarn entry that matches no diagnostic.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition=" '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'FSO.Client' ">
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS0436</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ============================================================================
    A-09 - the five 9.0.x servicing-band pins: DELIBERATELY NOT CHANGED
    ============================================================================
    The audit listed five Microsoft packages pinned to the 9.0 servicing band
    under a tree that targets net10.0, and suggested moving them to 10.0.x:

        System.Configuration.ConfigurationManager 9.0.9   (FSO.Server.Debug,
                                                           SimplePaletteQuantizer)
        System.Drawing.Common                     9.0.9   (FSO.UI, FSO.Server.Debug)
        System.Speech                             9.0.9   (FSO.Windows)
        System.Diagnostics.PerformanceCounter     9.0.9   (FSO.Server)
        Microsoft.Data.Sqlite.Core                9.0.9   (FSO.Server.Api.Core)

    The fix was checked and not applied, because the thing the finding is worried
    about - "they will not receive net10.0 servicing" - was measured to not apply
    to the software this repository ships.

    MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED
    =====================
    Simitone.Windows/obj/project.assets.json, for the post-B-12 game closure,
    resolves 52 libraries and NONE of them is any of the five. The assembly is
    already in the Microsoft.NETCore.App targeting pack that net10.0-windows
    brings in, so the SDK's package/file conflict resolution drops the package
    as framework-provided before it ever becomes a reference. (The same assets
    file does contain all five names, but only inside the targeting pack's own
    assembly list at version range (,10.0.32767] - which is what the .NET 10
    shared framework provides, not a resolution of the 9.0.9 package.)

    So on the shipped path these pins are as decorative as A-04's 8.0.0 and
    9.0.0 spellings: nothing is compiled against them, nothing is copied from
    them, and there is no servicing to receive. A 10.0.x pin would resolve to
    exactly the same framework assembly.

    WHY NOT ANYWAY
    ==============
    For the -p:BuildIde=true closure they are real, because those FreeSO
    projects target net9.0 and the packages are not pruned there. Bumping them
    was validated - a scratch net9.0 project restoring all five at 10.0.12
    succeeds, with no NU1202, and every one of them picks its lib/net9.0 asset
    (so this is not the netstandard2.0 downgrade it could have been) - but it
    also pulls a different transitive graph into the TSO server closure:
    Microsoft.Data.Sqlite.Core 9.0.9 -> 10.0.12 moves SQLitePCLRaw to 2.1.12 and
    System.Configuration.ConfigurationManager 9.0.9 -> 10.0.12 moves
    System.Security.Cryptography.ProtectedData, System.Threading.AccessControl
    and System.Win32.SystemEvents forward. That is a real change to code this
    repository does not own, does not ship, and cannot test, in exchange for
    nothing measurable in the artefact that is shipped.

    Upstream owns it. Same line as the A-05/A-07/A-10 items in the audit's
    section 6: five one-line PackageReference edits in a vendored submodule, for
    zero benefit to the game and a non-zero risk to the -ide build, is a change
    to make upstream rather than one to make here. If a FreeSO bump moves these
    to the 10.0 band on its own, nothing in this file contradicts it.
  -->

</Project>
